{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>When a man gives.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory established this pericope (Bava Metzi'a 94a) as referring to an unpaid bailee, for he alone is exempt for theft and loss. And they said that in any event he is liable for negligence, yet it is not known from where they derived this, since Scripture obligated him only to swear that he did not lay his hand upon it — beyond this he is exempt. And Rashi of blessed memory, in the passage beginning 'aשומר חנם on folio 95, wrote regarding negligence, and this is his language: 'For Scripture speaks of negligence in his case, as it is written ״עַל כָּל דְּבַר פֶּשַׁע״.' And it remains to be known on what basis Scripture is understood to be speaking of an unpaid bailee. Perhaps it is because He clothed the giving in silver or vessels — things that ordinarily do not require a fee to guard them — whereas in the case of a paid bailee He speaks of things that ordinarily require a fee to guard them, namely a donkey or an ox or a sheep and any animal, for every such type requires care and ordinarily the practice is to guard them for a fee." "<b>To his fellow.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory said (Bava Metzi'a 57a): and not [property] of consecrated [sanctuary]. And it appears to me to give a reason for this — for its own reason, that HaShem commanded to exempt [a paid bailee] when the owner [himself] is engaged in work for the bailee (בעלים). And it would be fitting to call HaShem the 'ba'alav' of the sanctuary together with [being] the 'ba'alav' of every deposit, for there is no hour or moment when HaShem is not performing an action with man, whether in respect of his body or in respect of his needs." "<b>Silver or vessels.</b> It should have needed to say only ״כִּי יִתֵּן אִישׁ אֶל רֵעֵהוּ״, and behold it says nearby ״עַל כָּל דְּבַר פֶּשַׁע עַל שׁוֹר עַל״ etc., and our Sages of blessed memory derived all the specific items for which he would be liable if he did not guard [them] and was negligent. And if the verse's intent were to record that it speaks of an unpaid bailee, it would have sufficed to say ״כִּי יִתֵּן כֶּסֶ״ף — why do I need [the word] 'vessels'? And in the Mekhilta they said: ״כֶּסֶף אוֹ כֵלִים״ — just as silver is normally counted, so vessels are normally counted; from here they said: any claim that is not by measure, by weight, or by count is not [a valid] claim — end quote. And there is no contradiction between our words and their words. It further appears to me [that there is] a reason for His saying <b>vessels</b>: to say that even if the bailee wishes to pay he is not exempt — as the Rambam wrote in chapter 6 of the Laws of Borrowing and Deposit, that if the deposit is a finished vessel they administer an oath to him [rather than allowing him to simply pay]. And even though the Rambam said this was by rabbinic enactment, it is possible that Scripture alludes to this. And it may also be derived from what the verse concludes saying, <b>if he did not lay his hand upon the work of his fellow</b> — it was precise in saying 'work,' to say that even if there is no [other] claim but rather a claim of workmanship in the vessels, he pays its value. Further, one may say that the verse specifies 'vessels' to include any claim involving vessels where the bailee cannot simply return the object but must pay — for a vessel's uniqueness lies in its craftsmanship, and once damaged it cannot be replaced by an equivalent." "<b>To guard.</b> In the Mekhilta, Rabbi Yishma'el says: [he is liable] only if he deposits it with him and says to him, 'Guard [it] for me'; but if he said to him, 'Your eyes [are] upon it' — behold, he is exempt. And in [the chapter] HaChovel (Bava Kamma 93a) they derived: 'to guard' — and not to lose or to tear; 'to guard' — and not to distribute to the poor. And from the words of the Tosafot there it appears that the Tanna did not exempt [him in] any case where he did not say to him 'Guard [it] for me' except from negligence; but if he lost it by hand [i.e., deliberately], he is not exempt unless he said to him explicitly to lose or to tear. And it is difficult for me: from where did they find [basis] to derive two exclusions at two levels — the first, if he did not say to him 'guard' he is exempt from negligence, and the second, if he said to him to lose [it], he is exempt if he lost [it]? And it appears that the derivation of the Mekhilta is a complete derashah, whereas the derivation of HaChovel, 'and not to lose,' is a logical inference — only they attached it to the verse. And know [this] from the fact that they further said 'and not to distribute to the poor,' and the reason for the exclusion regarding the poor is derived from there, for it is because it is money that has no claimants. As they said there in that story where a certain man consecrated his purse for charity — Rav Yosef held that man liable [when] thieves came and stole it. Abbaye said to him: 'But it was taught in a beraita: ״לִשְׁמֹר״ — and not to distribute to the poor!' He said to him: 'The poor of Pumbedita are disgusted with him.' And Rashi explained: it was money that had claimants. See, the reason is this [very point] — and for this reason no exclusion [from the verse] should have been needed, for once he consecrated this money to the poor, his rights in it were voided and the money remained without claimants. And it appears that the same judgment applies if he told him to guard [it in order] to distribute [it] to the poor — that he is exempt for the reason that it is money that has no claimants. And the proof is from what Abbaye challenges Rav Yosef from the beraita ״לִשְׁמֹר — וְלֹא לְחַלֵּק״, and what is the difficulty? Perhaps he explicitly told him to guard! What will you say — that he did not tell him to guard? If so, he should have challenged him from the beraita of ״לִשְׁמֹר״ — [that he is liable only] if he said to him 'guard,' and without this he would be exempt even [regarding] money that is not for distribution. They further said there in the Mekhilta, after the words of Rabbi Yishma'el: Rabbi Natan says: ״לִשְׁמֹר״ — to include everything similar to silver and vessels."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {1 "The argument turns on the word בעלים (ba'alav), which in rabbinic law means 'the owner' but literally means 'its master/lord'; Or HaChaim reads it as also applying to HaShem as the 'master' of consecrated property."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:32:53.984787104Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}